WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Basic Nature and Purpose of PFSS

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Welcome to this session.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.133
We’re going to be looking at a concept called PFSS,

4
00:00:13.133 --> 00:00:16.099
Processing for Six Sigma.

5
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:21.600
What does processing for Six Sigma mean? Well to begin to understand this.

6
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.800
Let's start with a simple process.

7
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:32.700
I'm going to say we've got process operation 1, which feeds operation 2,

8
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:37.000
which feeds operation 3 and so on,

9
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.000
till ultimately a product or service is created.

10
00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Well, this process operation 1 has a certain outcome.

11
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.900
I'm going to call it for the sake of argument, a deliverable.

12
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:59.866
So, what comes out of here is a deliverable. And we're going to blow that out right here.

13
00:00:59.866 --> 00:01:05.432
And look at that deliverable as a simple square.

14
00:01:05.433 --> 00:01:08.899
That's our deliverable.

15
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:12.900
Now, the deliverable coming out of here.

16
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:19.933
This deliverable went into operation 1. Had further processing done to it.

17
00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:25.899
In other words, value added activity occurred at operation 1.

18
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:32.833
That transformed this deliverable say from a state

19
00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:39.299
like this, to this state.

20
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.600
And I’m going to graphically illustrate that a little bit more,

21
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.033
just to kind of help us understand it.

22
00:01:47.033 --> 00:01:52.066
As if it was a building block. Say, this is what we put in.

23
00:01:52.066 --> 00:01:59.099
And this is what we got out.

24
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.900
So, you can see the green area has increased

25
00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:06.133
and now the dashed line has become a solid line.

26
00:02:06.133 --> 00:02:10.566
So the purpose of operation 1, was to add value.

27
00:02:10.566 --> 00:02:14.566
Increase the green area and make it a solid line.

28
00:02:14.566 --> 00:02:20.099
So, that operation did that. In this particular case, we have now declared

29
00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:24.100
two critical to quality characteristics.

30
00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:29.133
So, the green is a critical to quality characteristic

31
00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:35.233
and this line is a critical to quality characteristic of the product.

32
00:02:35.233 --> 00:02:39.466
Now, we recognize that at this operation.

33
00:02:39.466 --> 00:02:44.066
There is critical to process characteristic 1,

34
00:02:44.066 --> 00:02:50.499
through critical to process characteristic N. Where N is some large number.

35
00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:56.666
We further recognize that these are the knobs, and dials, and switches at this operation.

36
00:02:56.666 --> 00:03:02.632
The material variables, the knowledge of the operators, all of the things that are essential

37
00:03:02.633 --> 00:03:08.199
to transform the deliverable from this state,

38
00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:14.766
which I'll call state A to state B. A higher value state.

39
00:03:14.766 --> 00:03:22.099
So, if these things are set right. If their settings are correct.

40
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:28.766
Then we will get full entitlement as we transform in

41
00:03:28.766 --> 00:03:32.799
to operation 1 and that's what we get out.

42
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:36.833
In turn, that's what's fed into operation 2,

43
00:03:36.833 --> 00:03:43.799
and from that we get out

44
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:50.233
something of even a higher value state. That's what comes out.

45
00:03:50.233 --> 00:03:56.866
So, we put in. We get out. We feed back in. We get back out.

46
00:03:56.866 --> 00:04:01.532
And so it goes, as we progressively build.

47
00:04:01.533 --> 00:04:05.833
Now, we’re going to come back and talk a little bit more about this process,

48
00:04:05.833 --> 00:04:10.566
in relation to Processing for Six Sigma and what that means in just a minute.

49
00:04:10.566 --> 00:04:14.199
But, right now we just want to make the fundamental point

50
00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:20.700
that Y is a function of X1,…,

51
00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:26.100
to the Nth variable, the Nth X, where N is some large number.

52
00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:32.566
And, we recognize that some of these X’s constitute the vital few,

53
00:04:32.566 --> 00:04:35.799
and others constitute the trivial many.

54
00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:42.066
That if I find those vital few knobs, or as we call them CTP's.

55
00:04:42.066 --> 00:04:46.366
The vital few critical to process variables, knobs, dials, and switches.

56
00:04:46.366 --> 00:04:51.499
I isolate the correct settings for them. What's going to happen out here?

57
00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:57.133
This is going to get better. The CTQ's is going to get better.

58
00:04:57.133 --> 00:05:03.066
Which means that I get more transformational entitlement

59
00:05:03.066 --> 00:05:07.299
going from state A to state B to state C.

60
00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:13.900
As the process capability improves, the capability of these CTP's

61
00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:20.900
and this operation to transform this input deliverable to this output deliverable.

62
00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:26.033
That's what we’re talking about by Processing for Six Sigma.

63
00:05:26.033 --> 00:05:31.033
Thank you for choosing SSMI.

